\subsection{Pretrained LLM Representation Quality Under Visual Input}
\label{sec:ood_diagnostics}

A potential concern with the three-condition comparison (\S\ref{sec:model_data}) is that the pretrained Llama-2 backbone never encountered projected visual tokens during pretraining, so its representations of such input could be degenerate.
We evaluate three diagnostics to assess representation quality.
Activation magnitude grows from $16.5$ (L0) to $90.2$ (L31) for Llama-2 base, compared to $16.5 \to 51.7$ for the VLA-trained model; the pretrained LLM produces larger but non-exploding activations with no evidence of collapse.
Per-layer cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers decreases progressively from $1.0$ to $\approx 0.28$ at L25, reflecting gradual differentiation rather than catastrophic out-of-distribution behavior.
Top-10 principal component variance explained remains stable across all layers ($0.59$--$0.74$), with no low-rank collapse.
These diagnostics indicate that the pretrained LLM produces structured, full-rank representations of visual input despite never encountering projected visual tokens during pretraining, supporting the validity of the three-condition comparison.

