\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}
\label{sec:linear_probe}

To rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\text{params} \approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \to 7$, $N/\text{params} \approx 2.2$).
The inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while untrained declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).
The trained--untrained gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\Delta = +0.015$), indicating that VLA training reorganizes representations to make action information more linearly decodable.
Untrained deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$), confirming that MLP nonlinearity partially rescues degraded representations.
These results confirm that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts.

